WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.470
Our new classroom sensor said it was

2
00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.930
eighty-five degrees at seven in the

3
00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:07.210
morning.

4
00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.480
The head teacher nearly

5
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.120
sent everyone home.

6
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.600
One wrong number almost decided our day.

7
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.260
Today: data you can trust.

8
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.140
Today: logging data you can trust.

9
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.600
Start with a question, not a sensor.

10
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.760
Ours: does our classroom get

11
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:30.880
too hot after lunch?

12
00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.430
So we log the air temperature at desk

13
00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.210
height, in the shade, every ten minutes.

14
00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.320
A reading on its own is just a number.

15
00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.040
Thirty-one means nothing until

16
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.640
you add the unit, degrees

17
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:48.400
Celsius, the time and the place.

18
00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.930
So: time, unit and place,

19
00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:53.310
on every reading.

20
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.170
Before you trust a new sensor, check

21
00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.950
it beside a trusted thermometer, in

22
00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:01.450
the same spot, for an hour.

23
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.270
If it always reads one degree high,

24
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:06.390
note that and correct for it.

25
00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.480
If it wanders up and down, don't use it.

26
00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:11.990
Here's a day from our log.

27
00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:14.390
It climbs from twenty-six degrees

28
00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.050
to thirty-two after lunch.

29
00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.410
But at seven o'clock: eighty-five.

30
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.280
A popular sensor reads exactly that just

31
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.700
after it powers up: a classic glitch.

32
00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:27.410
Quick check!

33
00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:30.410
We know the eighty-five is a glitch.

34
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.760
What should we do with it?

35
00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:36.070
Leave it out, and write down why.

36
00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:40.110
Keep the raw log exactly as it was, and

37
00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.410
note what you removed: seven o'clock,

38
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:45.110
eighty-five, sensor just powered up.

39
00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:47.390
Then anyone can check your cleaning.

40
00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:49.680
Why does it matter?

41
00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:52.550
With the glitch, the average of those

42
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.110
eight readings is about thirty-six

43
00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.750
degrees, hotter than any real reading.

44
00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:00.230
Without it, about twenty-nine.

45
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:04.030
The median, the middle value, is thirty

46
00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:06.790
with the glitch and twenty-nine without.

47
00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.730
To find the median, put the readings in

48
00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.390
order and take the middle one.

49
00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:14.770
With an even number, take

50
00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:16.770
halfway between the middle two.

51
00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:20.390
One wild reading moves it only one place.

52
00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:22.260
Your turn.

53
00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.490
My pupils' week of readings has two

54
00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:27.030
glitches they can't explain.

55
00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:30.450
They want one fair number for a typical

56
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.010
afternoon: the median, or the average?

57
00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:37.510
The median: a few wild readings barely

58
00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.630
move it, but drag the average.

59
00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:43.330
Remember: start with a question, put a

60
00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.210
time, unit and place on every reading,

61
00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:48.870
check against a reference, and clean

62
00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:49.290
openly.

63
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.560
Try it in class tomorrow: a

64
00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.660
paper log on a rota.

65
00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.120
Each hour, a pupil reads a

66
00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.620
thermometer in the shade and

67
00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:03.880
writes the time, reading and unit.

68
00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.110
Then find the median together.

69
00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:11.210
Next time: rules that act on data.
